.TH DU 1 
.SH NAME
du  \-  summarize disk usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B du
[
.B \-s
] [
.B \-a
] [ name ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Du
gives the number of blocks contained in all files
and (recursively) directories within each specified directory or
file
.IR name .
If
.I name
is missing,
`\fB.\fR'
is used.
.PP
The optional argument
.B \-s
causes only the grand total to
be given.
The optional argument
.B \-a
causes an entry to be generated
for each file.
Absence of either causes an entry to be generated for
each directory only.
.PP
A file which has two links to it is only counted once.
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
the block
.I du
counts in is
.B 1024
bytes.
It is not this machine's filesystem block, which is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- and is three of them.
.PP
Two things follow.
Every number
.I du
prints is a
.B multiple of three,
the smallest thing that can be allocated being one 3072-byte block: a one-byte file
costs three.
And the numbers are
.B half
what a PDP\-11 printed, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks \- not a sixth, which is what
they were when this program counted in the filesystem block.
.PP
.B "Note:"
there is no
.I BSHIFT
on this machine and there cannot be, 3072 not being a power of two; the conversion is a
divide by the filesystem block and then a multiply, and both live in
.IR sys/param.h .
.PP
.IR Df (1M),
.IR quot (1M)
and
.IR ls (1)
report in the same block.
.SH BUGS
Non-directories
given as arguments (not under
.B \-a
option) are not listed.
.br
If there are too many distinct linked files,
.I du
counts the excess files multiply.
.br
.B "Note:"
a path longer than 256 characters, or one grown past that by the walk, is refused with
.B "--path too long"
and skipped.
v7 appended one component per directory level into a fixed buffer with no test of any kind.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
df(1M), quot(1M), ls(1)
